Date: Fri, 11 Sep 2026 08:12:23 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 11/25] Make the Krama engine's output epistemically auditable,
 fix two real bugs
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Responds to a second independent review of the first regenerated-output
run, which argued the deeper problem was not individual wrong strings but
that the JSON gave no way to tell canonical output from a guess, or a
checked fact from an unchecked one.

Checked the review's specific count-bug claim directly against the data:
both pada_count and pair_count were already internally consistent (a 10.3
tri-unit legitimately replaces one ordinary pair at its boundary). The
underlying complaint was fair though -- the arithmetic tying them together
was nowhere visible. build_counts() now asserts this invariant and ships
the formula in an "invariant_check" object instead of bare numbers.

Every unit now carries independent status ("canonical" vs
"candidate_reconstruction", so a single cited worked-example
generalization like sutra 10.3's can never be mistaken for verified
output) alongside confidence (unchanged: classifier certainty, not
correctness). Parigraha units state their retake_state (STHITOPASTHITA)
and named trigger_reasons instead of a bare sutra-number list.
compare_against_old() keys by (ardharca_index, unit_index_within_ardharca)
so one real difference no longer cascades into spurious downstream ones.

Replaced a first, flawed attempt at attestation (comparing isolated
2-word Krama pairs against continuous samhita text via substring match --
wrong, because a word's pair reflects only its immediate partner while
its continuous-text form is often governed by a different, later
neighbour) with sanskrit_phonology.reconstruct_chain(): folds the sandhi
engine across a WHOLE ardharca to simulate how continuous text is built,
then diffs against DGE's attested samhita_patha per ardharca.

Building that check properly caught two real, now-fixed phonology bugs:
- resolve_compound() misfiring on a chained string's own elision-avagraha
  (ordinary Sanskrit orthography, not a Pada-patha compound marker),
  collapsing every space built up so far. Fixed via samhita_join's new
  resolve_w1_compound parameter.
- The lexical-exception tables only matched a bare word, silently never
  firing for that word occurring anywhere but the start of a chain.
  Fixed via _match_lexical_suffix().

And precisely diagnosed, rather than patched, one further real bug: the
indic_transliteration library's SLP1 scheme cannot distinguish "र्ऋ"
(bare consonant, independent vowel) from "रृ" (dependent vowel-matra) --
both collapse to the same SLP1 string, so a fix that survives one join
loses the distinction on a second. Chain reconstruction now matches DGE's
attested text on 16 of 18 RV 1.1 ardharcas (up from an unverified 69/70
claim made in an earlier, uncommitted ad hoc check).

Architecture doc sec.6.7 and PENDING.md record what the review correctly
flagged as still missing (a real Patala-11 Kramahetu evaluation layer,
a restore_* family, structured Svara, full per-unit transformation
history) and explicitly not attempted this pass, plus the review's
standing acceptance criterion recorded verbatim in sec.10.

